rlm-git-update [-n|--dry-run] [-b|--branch <name>] [<directory>]

Switch every git repo in a directory to the target branch (default
main) and hard-reset that branch to exactly origin/<branch>. Written
for the multi-repo collection dirs that rlm-git-status reports on —
one directory holding a checkout of every repo — where the routine
task is "get all of these back to a clean copy of the remote".

The target branch is treated as a read-only mirror of the remote.
That is the intent, but it means unpushed commits on that branch are
destroyed, so the function is deliberately conservative about what it
will touch and loud about what it discards.

Scans the immediate children of <directory> (default: the current
directory), treating each child that is its own git worktree root as
a member. When the target has no repo children it falls back to the
enclosing repo, so the function also works from inside a single
worktree at any depth.

SAFETY
  Repos with uncommitted changes to *tracked* files are skipped and
  listed — reset --hard would destroy those edits. Untracked files do
  not block the run, since reset leaves them alone.

  Commits that exist on the local branch but not on origin are listed
  before being discarded, so nothing is thrown away silently. They
  remain recoverable via `git reflog`.

  Stashes are never touched (reset does not affect refs/stash), and
  neither are other branches. A branch left behind that has no
  upstream is reported, so local-only work stays visible.

  Each repo is fetched before it is reset. Resetting to a stale
  cached origin/<branch> would report success while pinning the repo
  to an old commit.

A per-repo log is followed by a summary counting updated, skipped and
failed repos, plus sections listing anything skipped, left behind
without an upstream, or discarded. The function returns 1 when any
repo failed, otherwise 0.

Colors are emitted only when stdout is a terminal, so the log stays
pasteable when piped or redirected.

OPTIONS
  -n, --dry-run       Report what would happen without changing
                      anything. No fetch is performed, so the discard
                      report is computed against a possibly stale
                      origin/<branch>.
  -b, --branch <name> Use <name> instead of main as the target
                      branch.
  -h, --help          Print a usage line.

EXAMPLES
  git-update                      # every repo under the current dir
  git-update -n                   # dry run: report, change nothing
  git-update ~/git/work/Platform  # a collection dir, from anywhere
  git-update -b devel             # target a different branch

REQUIRES
  git
